IMPORT prelude

-- Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993 (NSW) -- the whole operative Act (ss 3-7), from the seat of the claimant.
--
-- SEAT: the person bringing a claim before a New South Wales court (or an arbitrator) where the claim is governed by the
-- law of another place. Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Does this Act apply to my proceedings?",
-- "Is the other place's time-limit law a limitation law?", "Will the court apply the other place's time limit to my
-- claim?", "How must the court use a power to extend time under that law?", "Does the other place's time limit, applied
-- through section 5, stop my claim?". The court gets no seat: what it must do (ss 5, 6) is encoded as what it means for
-- the claimant.
--
-- Provisions: s 3 (court, limitation law); s 4 (application); s 5 (limitation laws are substantive); s 6 (discretions
-- under those laws); s 7 (New Zealand). Out: s 1 (name), s 2 (commencement, except that its date is asked as an input for
-- s 4), s 8 (the Minister's review): machinery.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", a flat "does not apply", an "until" period), or NOTHING where the Act does not answer on
-- the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment. The Act decides only whose limitation law a NSW court
-- applies when another Australian place's (or New Zealand's) law governs; it says nothing about which law governs, and
-- nothing about claims governed by NSW law or by the law of a country other than New Zealand.
--
-- Dates use only the built-in date functions (DATE_FROM_DMY, DATE_SERIAL), not the daydate library.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Choice of Law (Limitation Periods) Act 1993`

§§ `The nouns`

-- "a place, being another State, a Territory or New Zealand" (ss 5, 6, 7). "the State" is New South Wales. Which law
-- governs the claim (the substantive law) is decided by the general law of choice of law, not by this Act: it is asked.
DECLARE `Place whose law governs` IS ONE OF
    `New South Wales`
    `another Australian State`
    `an Australian Territory`
    `New Zealand`
    `a country other than New Zealand`

-- "a court of the State" (ss 5, 6), with s 3 "court includes arbitrator". Whether an arbitrator is "of the State" is not
-- said: an arbitration held in NSW is taken as one, an arbitration held elsewhere is left open (fork F2).
DECLARE `Where my claim is heard` IS ONE OF
    `a court of New South Wales`
    `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in New South Wales`
    `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held outside New South Wales`
    `a court outside New South Wales`

-- The answer to "does the other place's time limit stop my claim?" (s 5 with that place's limitation law).
DECLARE `What section 5 means for my time limit` IS ONE OF
    `section 5 applies, and I started in time under that place's limitation law`
    `section 5 applies, and the time that place's limitation law allows has passed`
    `section 5 does not apply to my claim`
    `this Act does not settle it`

-- The parties and acts of the one regulative rule.
DECLARE `Claim party` IS ONE OF `the claimant`
DECLARE `Claim act` IS ONE OF `institute proceedings on the claim`

§§ `Dates`

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the calendar date` d m y MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY d m y

GIVEN `the first day` IS A DATE, `the second day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the first day` `is before` `the second day` IF DATE_SERIAL `the first day` LESS THAN DATE_SERIAL `the second day`

GIVEN `the start` IS A DATE, `the end` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days from` `the start` `to` `the end` MEANS DATE_SERIAL `the end` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the start`

§§ `s 3 -- definitions`

-- s 3 "limitation law means a law that provides for the limitation or exclusion of any liability or the barring of a
-- right of action in respect of a claim by reference to the time when a proceeding on, or the arbitration of, the claim
-- is commenced." A "means" definition: when it fails, JUST FALSE.
@export Is this time-limit law of the other place a "limitation law" under the Act (s 3)?
GIVEN `that law limits or excludes liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law you are asking about limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, in respect of a claim
      `it does so by reference to when proceedings or an arbitration on the claim are commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it does so by reference to the time when court proceedings on the claim, or an arbitration of it, are started (as a limitation period does)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it a limitation law` MEANS
    IF     `that law limits or excludes liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim`
       AND `it does so by reference to when proceedings or an arbitration on the claim are commenced`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- "a place, being another State, a Territory or New Zealand".
GIVEN `the place` IS A `Place whose law governs`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is another State, a Territory or New Zealand` `the place` IF
    CONSIDER `the place`
    WHEN `another Australian State` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an Australian Territory` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `New Zealand` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `ss 4, 7 -- which proceedings the Act applies to`

-- s 4 "This Act extends to a cause of action that arose before the commencement of this Act, but does not apply to
-- proceedings instituted before the commencement of this Act." s 7(1) "This Act does not apply in relation to New Zealand
-- until it is declared by proclamation ... that it does so apply." s 7(2) "If the substantive law of New Zealand is to
-- govern a claim before a court of the State and proceedings have been instituted on the claim before that declaration
-- takes effect, this Act does not apply to those proceedings. This subsection has effect despite section 4."
-- Flat negatives and an "until" period: JUST FALSE. Proceedings instituted on the commencement day, or on the day the
-- declaration takes effect, are not "before" it (fork F1). For an arbitration, "proceedings instituted" is read as the
-- arbitration commenced (fork F3). The date of commencement (fixed by proclamation under s 2) and the New Zealand
-- declaration are not deposited: they are inputs.
@export Does the Act apply to my proceedings, given when they were started (ss 4, 7)?
GIVEN `the place whose law governs my claim` IS A `Place whose law governs` @desc The place whose substantive law governs your claim. One of: "New South Wales", "another Australian State", "an Australian Territory", "New Zealand", "a country other than New Zealand"
      `the day my proceedings were instituted` IS A DATE @desc The day you started the court proceedings (or the arbitration) on the claim
      `the day this Act commenced` IS A DATE @desc The day the Act commenced, as fixed by proclamation under s 2 (not deposited here; check the NSW legislation website)
      `the Act has been declared to apply to New Zealand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proclamation published on the NSW legislation website has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 7(1))
      `the day that declaration took effect` IS A DATE @desc The day that New Zealand declaration took effect (any day if there is none)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does this Act apply to my proceedings` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the day my proceedings were instituted` `is before` `the day this Act commenced` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the place whose law governs my claim` EQUALS `New Zealand`
           THEN IF (NOT `the Act has been declared to apply to New Zealand`)
                THEN JUST FALSE
                ELSE IF `the day my proceedings were instituted` `is before` `the day that declaration took effect`
                     THEN JUST FALSE
                     ELSE JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The forum test of ss 5 and 6, "a court of the State" with s 3 "court includes arbitrator".
GIVEN `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`is a court of the State` `the forum` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the forum`
    WHEN `a court of New South Wales` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in New South Wales` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `an arbitrator, in an arbitration held outside New South Wales` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: "of the State" is not said of an arbitrator; fork F2)
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- The conditions ss 5 and 6 share: a listed place, the Act applying in time (ss 4, 7), a limitation law (s 3), and a
-- court of the State. Every condition is introduced by "if" or is a "means" definition, so a failed one is JUST FALSE;
-- a failed condition beats an open one.
GIVEN `the place` IS A `Place whose law governs`
      `the forum` IS A `Where my claim is heard`
      `instituted` IS A DATE
      `commenced` IS A DATE
      `declared for New Zealand` IS A BOOLEAN
      `declaration day` IS A DATE
      `limits or bars` IS A BOOLEAN
      `by reference to commencement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the shared conditions of ss 5 and 6 hold for` `the place` `the forum` `instituted` `commenced` `declared for New Zealand` `declaration day` `limits or bars` `by reference to commencement` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `is another State, a Territory or New Zealand` `the place`) THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `Does this Act apply to my proceedings` `the place` `instituted` `commenced` `declared for New Zealand` `declaration day` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `Is it a limitation law` `limits or bars` `by reference to commencement` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
           OTHERWISE `is a court of the State` `the forum`

§§ `s 5 -- the other place's limitation law is part of its substantive law`

-- s 5 "If the substantive law of a place, being another State, a Territory or New Zealand, is to govern a claim before a
-- court of the State, a limitation law of that place is to be regarded as part of that substantive law and applied
-- accordingly by the court." The duty is the court's; for the claimant it means the other place's time limit, not
-- NSW's, is applied to the claim.
@export Will the NSW court (or arbitrator) treat the other place's time-limit law as part of the law governing my claim and apply it (s 5)?
GIVEN `the place whose law governs my claim` IS A `Place whose law governs` @desc The place whose substantive law governs your claim. One of: "New South Wales", "another Australian State", "an Australian Territory", "New Zealand", "a country other than New Zealand"
      `where my claim is heard` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is being decided. One of: "a court of New South Wales", "an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in New South Wales", "an arbitrator, in an arbitration held outside New South Wales", "a court outside New South Wales"
      `the day my proceedings were instituted` IS A DATE @desc The day you started the court proceedings (or the arbitration) on the claim
      `the day this Act commenced` IS A DATE @desc The day the Act commenced, as fixed by proclamation under s 2 (not deposited here; check the NSW legislation website)
      `the Act has been declared to apply to New Zealand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proclamation published on the NSW legislation website has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 7(1))
      `the day that declaration took effect` IS A DATE @desc The day that New Zealand declaration took effect (any day if there is none)
      `that law limits or excludes liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other place's law you are asking about limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, in respect of a claim
      `it does so by reference to when proceedings or an arbitration on the claim are commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it does so by reference to the time when proceedings on the claim, or an arbitration of it, are started
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Will the court apply the other place's limitation law` MEANS
    `the shared conditions of ss 5 and 6 hold for` `the place whose law governs my claim` `where my claim is heard` `the day my proceedings were instituted` `the day this Act commenced` `the Act has been declared to apply to New Zealand` `the day that declaration took effect` `that law limits or excludes liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim` `it does so by reference to when proceedings or an arbitration on the claim are commenced`

-- The limitation period of the other place, as s 5 applies it, written as a rule: the claimant must institute
-- proceedings by the last day that place's limitation law allows. (Jurally the claimant is under no duty; the law
-- creates a liability to have the claim barred. The rule models that as LEST BREACH.) Time 0 is the day the cause of
-- action arose; the period's length and its counting rules are that place's, and the last day is asked as a fact.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Claim party` `Claim act`
`s 5 -- the claimant must institute proceedings within the period the other place's limitation law allows` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `institute proceedings on the claim`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "proceedings instituted after the last day the governing place's limitation law allows (s 5)"

-- s 4 "extends to a cause of action that arose before the commencement of this Act": the day the cause of action arose
-- does not affect whether the Act applies; it is the start of the other place's period.
@export Does the other place's time limit, applied by the NSW court under section 5, stop my claim (ss 4, 5)?
GIVEN `the place whose law governs my claim` IS A `Place whose law governs` @desc The place whose substantive law governs your claim. One of: "New South Wales", "another Australian State", "an Australian Territory", "New Zealand", "a country other than New Zealand"
      `where my claim is heard` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is being decided. One of: "a court of New South Wales", "an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in New South Wales", "an arbitrator, in an arbitration held outside New South Wales", "a court outside New South Wales"
      `the day my proceedings were instituted` IS A DATE @desc The day you started the court proceedings (or the arbitration) on the claim
      `the day this Act commenced` IS A DATE @desc The day the Act commenced, as fixed by proclamation under s 2 (not deposited here; check the NSW legislation website)
      `the Act has been declared to apply to New Zealand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proclamation published on the NSW legislation website has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 7(1))
      `the day that declaration took effect` IS A DATE @desc The day that New Zealand declaration took effect (any day if there is none)
      `that law limits or excludes liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other place's time-limit law limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, in respect of a claim
      `it does so by reference to when proceedings or an arbitration on the claim are commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it does so by reference to the time when proceedings on the claim, or an arbitration of it, are started
      `the day my cause of action arose` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action arose (it may be before the Act commenced)
      `the last day that law allows me to start` IS A DATE @desc The last day on which the other place's limitation law allows proceedings on your claim to be started, counting by that law's own rules and including any extension the court has granted under it
GIVETH A `What section 5 means for my time limit`
`Does the other place's time limit stop my claim` MEANS
    CONSIDER `Will the court apply the other place's limitation law` `the place whose law governs my claim` `where my claim is heard` `the day my proceedings were instituted` `the day this Act commenced` `the Act has been declared to apply to New Zealand` `the day that declaration took effect` `that law limits or excludes liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim` `it does so by reference to when proceedings or an arbitration on the claim are commenced`
    WHEN JUST b THEN
        IF b
        THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 5 -- the claimant must institute proceedings within the period the other place's limitation law allows` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day started`), (EVENT `the claimant` `institute proceedings on the claim` `the day started`))
             WHEN FULFILLED THEN `section 5 applies, and I started in time under that place's limitation law`
             OTHERWISE `section 5 applies, and the time that place's limitation law allows has passed`
        ELSE `section 5 does not apply to my claim`
    OTHERWISE `this Act does not settle it` -- NOTHING-kind (the text is silent: an arbitrator outside NSW; fork F2)
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `the day my cause of action arose` `to` `the last day that law allows me to start`
        `the day started` MEANS `the days from` `the day my cause of action arose` `to` `the day my proceedings were instituted`

§§ `s 6 -- a discretion under the other place's limitation law`

-- s 6 "If a court of the State exercises a discretion conferred under a limitation law of a place, being another State, a
-- Territory or New Zealand, that discretion, as far as practicable, is to be exercised in the manner in which it is
-- exercised in comparable cases by the courts of that place." How far it is practicable is the court's judgment: the
-- answer JUST TRUE is the direction, "as far as practicable", not a prediction of the outcome.
@export If the NSW court decides whether to extend my time under the other place's limitation law, must it do so as that place's courts do in comparable cases, as far as practicable (s 6)?
GIVEN `the place whose limitation law it is` IS A `Place whose law governs` @desc The place whose limitation law gives the court the discretion (for example a power to extend time). One of: "New South Wales", "another Australian State", "an Australian Territory", "New Zealand", "a country other than New Zealand"
      `where my claim is heard` IS A `Where my claim is heard` @desc Where your claim is being decided. One of: "a court of New South Wales", "an arbitrator, in an arbitration held in New South Wales", "an arbitrator, in an arbitration held outside New South Wales", "a court outside New South Wales"
      `the day my proceedings were instituted` IS A DATE @desc The day you started the court proceedings (or the arbitration) on the claim
      `the day this Act commenced` IS A DATE @desc The day the Act commenced, as fixed by proclamation under s 2 (not deposited here; check the NSW legislation website)
      `the Act has been declared to apply to New Zealand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a proclamation published on the NSW legislation website has declared that the Act applies in relation to New Zealand (s 7(1))
      `the day that declaration took effect` IS A DATE @desc The day that New Zealand declaration took effect (any day if there is none)
      `that law limits or excludes liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that place's law limits or excludes a liability, or bars a right of action, in respect of a claim
      `it does so by reference to when proceedings or an arbitration on the claim are commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it does so by reference to the time when proceedings on the claim, or an arbitration of it, are started
      `the court is exercising a discretion that law confers` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is exercising a discretion conferred by that limitation law (for example deciding your application to extend the time)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the court use the discretion as that place's courts do` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the court is exercising a discretion that law confers`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE `the shared conditions of ss 5 and 6 hold for` `the place whose limitation law it is` `where my claim is heard` `the day my proceedings were instituted` `the day this Act commenced` `the Act has been declared to apply to New Zealand` `the day that declaration took effect` `that law limits or excludes liability, or bars a right of action, on a claim` `it does so by reference to when proceedings or an arbitration on the claim are commenced`
